@node Why does beads.el require the bd CLI?
@subsection Why does beads.el require the bd CLI?
@cindex bd CLI requirement

beads.el is a frontend.  All data operations go through @command{bd},
which manages the Dolt database, so the Emacs UI and command-line
workflows always agree.

@node How do I use beads.el with remote projects?
@subsection How do I use beads.el with remote projects?
@cindex remote projects

Open beads.el from a TRAMP directory and it acts on that host's store.
Synchronous commands use @code{process-file}.  Asynchronous reads on a
single-hop ssh-family method run as local @command{ssh -T} processes that
@command{cd} to the store, so they never block Emacs and never share
TRAMP's pty-backed master.  ssh runs in BatchMode, so key or agent
authentication is required.  Opening, rendering, and folding a view
perform no file access over TRAMP (@pxref{Terminal Backends}).

@node How do I add a command that is not covered yet?
@subsection How do I add a command that is not covered yet?
@cindex adding a command

Define a @code{beads-defcommand} class in a new
@file{beads-command-<name>.el} file and declare its result type; the CLI
serialization, transient menu, and JSON parsing are derived from the slot
metadata (@pxref{EIEIO Command Classes}).  When the command belongs to a
top-level @command{bd} group, add a leaf class and bind it from the
group's parent transient rather than defining a class for the router.

@node Where do I report a bug?
@subsection Where do I report a bug?
@cindex bug reports

Use the tracker for the @command{bd} CLI for data problems and the
beads.el tracker for UI problems.  Include @kbd{M-x beads-version} output
for version skew.
